          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 10: Solve 28 $(x + 4) + 300 = 1000.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.275,
            0.571,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "Here are some ways to solve this equation."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.305,
            0.885,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Subtracting 300 from both sides, we get  $28\\left (x+4  \\right ) =1000-300$   $28\\left (x+4  \\right ) =700.$  Dividing both sides by 28, we get  $x + 4 = 700 \\div 28$   $x + 4 = 25.$  So  $x = 2 5 - 4 ,$  , which gives  $x = 2 1 .$  no</td><td rowspan=\"2\"> $(x + 4) + 300 = 1000$  28 Since 28, 300, and 1000 are divisible by 4, we get a simpler equation if we divide both sides by 4.  $\\frac{28(x + 4) + 300}{4} = \\frac{1000}{4}$  Using the rules of fraction addition, we get  $\\frac{28(x + 4)}{4} + \\frac{300}{4} = \\frac{1000}{4}$   $7(x + 4) + 75 = 250$  Subtracting 75 from both sides,  $7(x + 4) = 175$   $7x + 28 = 175$  Subtracting 28 from both sides,  $7 x = 1 4 7$   $x = \\frac{147}{7} = 21.$ </td><td rowspan=\"2\"> $28(x + 4) + 300 = 1000$  Simplifying the LHS,  $28x+112+300=1000$  V  $28x + 412 = 1000$  Subtracting 412 from both sides, we get  $28x = 1000 - 412$   $2 8 x = 5 8 8$  Dividing both sides by  $x = 588 \\div 28$ </td></tr><tr><td>28,  $x = 2 1 .$ </td></tr></table>",
